WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.340
Adventure has a name! Or, actually, it has&nbsp;
eight. From the man himself to a literal baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.000
these are the actors who have played the&nbsp;
legendary archaeologist Indiana Jones.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.040
Harrison Ford first appeared as Indiana&nbsp;
Jones in 1981's Raiders of the Lost Ark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.020
instantly cementing the character's reputation as&nbsp;
a cinematic legend. After Raiders, Ford appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.760
in four more Jones adventures: Temple of Doom,&nbsp;
The Last Crusade, Kingdom of the Crystal Skull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.140
and Dial of Destiny. Ford is more or less&nbsp;
synonymous with Indy, which is all the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.120
remarkable considering George Lucas and Steven&nbsp;
Spielberg initially cast Tom Selleck for the part.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.960
"Look, I did what I did, I don't&nbsp;
expect you to be happy about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.700
Thought maybe it could do us both some good."

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.200
Selleck was too busy racing around&nbsp;
Hawaii in a red Ferrari on Magnum P.I.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.300
however, and Ford landed the role instead.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.560
Of course, Indiana Jones is just one of the&nbsp;
iconic characters Harrison Ford has played in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.120
his illustrious career. Before Raiders, he had&nbsp;
portrayed another of George Lucas' swaggering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.580
hot-shot rogues: Han Solo in Star Wars. He also&nbsp;
starred as Rick Deckard in the dystopian sci-fi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.740
classic Blade Runner, played Dr. Richard Kimble&nbsp;
in The Fugitive, kicked butt as US President James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.360
Marshall in Air Force One… the list is nearly&nbsp;
endless, in fact, and in the years since Raiders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.160
Ford has become one of Hollywood's most iconic&nbsp;
actors. Not too shabby for an ex-carpenter.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.380
River Phoenix plays a young Indiana Jones in the&nbsp;
opening sequence of The Last Crusade. In the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.060
installment of the franchise, boy scout Indy takes&nbsp;
on a gang of grave robbers bent on stealing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.560
golden crucifix for a mysterious benefactor.&nbsp;
Racing through the rugged Utah landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.460
Indy does his best to thwart the thieves&nbsp;
— but his efforts prove unsuccessful.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.260
"You lost today, kid. But that&nbsp;
doesn't mean you have to like it."

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.540
While they don't share any&nbsp;
scenes in The Last Crusade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.420
this wasn't the first time Phoenix&nbsp;
had crossed paths with Harrison Ford;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.380
he had previously played Ford's&nbsp;
son in 1986's The Mosquito Coast.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.900
By the time Phoenix took on&nbsp;
the role of Indiana Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.040
he was already one of Hollywood's most&nbsp;
promising young stars. He starred in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.180
1986 coming-of-age classic Stand&nbsp;
By Me, and, at just 17 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.800
delivered an Oscar-nominated performance in&nbsp;
Running On Empty. Tragically, Phoenix died in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.680
1993 at age 23 from a drug overdose. At the time&nbsp;
of Phoenix's death, Ford told the New York Times:

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.500
"He played my son once, and I&nbsp;
came to love him like a son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.940
and was proud to watch him grow into a man&nbsp;
of such talent and integrity and compassion."

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.260
Veteran stage actor George Hall played&nbsp;
the elderly Indiana Jones in The Young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.180
Indiana Jones Chronicles. Episodes of The Young&nbsp;
Indiana Jones Chronicles are framed by old Indy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.740
memories and he serves as narrator for the show's&nbsp;
adventures — although this older Indy was edited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.580
out of the series when George Lucas re-cut&nbsp;
it as The Adventures of Young Indiana Jones.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.260
Indiana Jones came late in Hall's decades-long&nbsp;
acting career. He trained at the Neighborhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.100
Playhouse School and worked in vaudeville&nbsp;
before hitting the Broadway stage in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.520
1940s. Hall died in 2002 at the age of 85, having&nbsp;
performed on stage just weeks before his death.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.040
Sean Patrick Flanery played a young Indy in the&nbsp;
'90s series The Young Indiana Jones Chronicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.020
as well as several TV movies. George Lucas&nbsp;
hatched the idea for a prequel series as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.860
a way to answer fans' questions about&nbsp;
the character. He cast Flanery as Indy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.220
because he embodied the enthusiastic&nbsp;
innocence that Lucas was looking for.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.260
"Oh, I've got a bad feeling about this."

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.940
And while Lucas intended The Young Indiana Jones&nbsp;
Chronicles to educate as much as entertain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.120
there's still plenty of action&nbsp;
and adventure to be had. Flanery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.420
a former autocross racer and&nbsp;
triathlete, threw himself into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.200
physically demanding role of young Indy,&nbsp;
even performing most of his own stunts.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.460
The role of Indiana Jones kicked off Flanery's&nbsp;
Hollywood career, which quickly moved into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.980
lead-role status for 1995's fantasy drama Powder.&nbsp;
He made a name for himself as a gritty action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.600
star, and is today best known for his part in&nbsp;
The Boondock Saints. As for returning as Indiana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.060
Jones? Well, Flanery certainly wouldn't turn down&nbsp;
the opportunity. As he told PopCulture in 2021:

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.300
"Man, I can probably count on one&nbsp;
hand the amount of people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.640
wouldn't want to do something&nbsp;
that George Lucas originated."

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.500
Corey Carrier played an even younger Indiana&nbsp;
Jones in The Young Indiana Jones Chronicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.240
depicting Indy as a globe-trotting nine-year-old.&nbsp;
Under the supervision of his parents, Henry Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.200
Sr. and Anna Jones, Carrier's version&nbsp;
of young Indy takes in the wonders of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.220
the world — and gets into a little trouble&nbsp;
along the way. Even young Indy didn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.120
to be locked away in the classroom for too&nbsp;
long, and his formative experiences offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.660
some welcome context for the adult version&nbsp;
of Indiana Jones we've all grown to love.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.220
Carrier maintained an active acting career in&nbsp;
the '90s, including roles as a young Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.280
Nixon in director Oliver Stone's 1995 film Nixon&nbsp;
and Pinocchio's mischievous chum Lampwick in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.300
Adventures of Pinocchio. Since then, Carrier&nbsp;
has been living his life mostly off-screen.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.640
Yes, we're including the baby, too. Credited&nbsp;
for just a single episode, Neil Boulane played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.540
an infant version of Indiana Jones in The&nbsp;
Indiana Jones Chronicles. Little Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.580
grows up after several scenes, ready to take on&nbsp;
more mature, childhood adventures. Albeit brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.720
Boulane's performance gives some important&nbsp;
context to a famous part of Indiana Jones lore.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.500
"You know how it is when you like someone&nbsp;
the first time you ever meet them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.280
I mean, really like them? Well, that's&nbsp;
how it was with me and Indiana."

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.560
Boulane only has one other credit to his&nbsp;
name: playing baby Jesus in 1995's Marie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.400
de Nazareth. As he seems to have retired from&nbsp;
acting around the same time that he grew teeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.760
it's perhaps no surprise that Boulane's&nbsp;
current whereabouts remain unknown.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.020
Chris Strompolos' depiction of Indiana&nbsp;
Jones might not be in the official canon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:15.000
but his tribute movie, Raiders of the Lost Ark:&nbsp;
The Adaptation, has become a legendary feat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.860
film fandom. As a 10-year-old living in Gulfport,&nbsp;
Mississippi, Strompolos and his friend dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.360
much of their childhood to remaking Raiders of the&nbsp;
Lost Ark scene-for-scene, an undertaking that took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.800
seven years to complete. As to why Strompolos made&nbsp;
the project, he later told the New York Times:

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.100
"I just wanted to be Indiana Jones.&nbsp;
I wanted to see what it would be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.620
to be so self-sufficient and&nbsp;
get in and out of trouble."

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:39.900
In the years after its 1989 completion, Raiders&nbsp;
of the Lost Ark: The Adaptation garnered a cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.160
following. These days, Strompolos has traded&nbsp;
in his Indy fedora and whip for a computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.000
with his LinkedIn page suggesting&nbsp;
that he works in animation and VFX.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.060
Welcome to the dregs: Tony Cox plays Indiana&nbsp;
Jones in 2008's Hollywood spoof Disaster Movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.100
joining a long tradition of pop culture parodies&nbsp;
of the iconic hero. Indiana Jones parodies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.880
nothing new, of course, and Cox's portrayal of&nbsp;
the man in the hat isn't all that groundbreaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.980
His raunchy take on the character suits the vibe&nbsp;
of Disaster Movie to a tee, but that's hardly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:13.140
compliment considering the movie currently sits&nbsp;
at a paltry 1% on Rotten Tomatoes' Tomatometer.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.940
"Indiana my ass!"

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.180
Still, Cox's appearance in such an&nbsp;
atrocious movie doesn't mean he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.180
not a fine actor. With film&nbsp;
credits dating back to 1980,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.620
Cox has scored parts in movies ranging&nbsp;
from Willow to Beetlejuice to Bad Santa.

